<!-- Description
Scenario:

Your team has built a custom Kubernetes Operator to manage AppConfig resources. The Operator reads the developer's desired state from the spec, provisions the underlying infrastructure, and then attempts to update the status.phase of the Custom Resource to "Ready" so the developers know it finished successfully.

However, the pipeline is failing. The Operator pod is running, but the AppConfig status remains empty. You check the Operator's logs and see a continuous stream of 403 Forbidden errors. The junior engineer who wrote the RBAC manifests insists they granted the operator-sa ServiceAccount full get, list, watch, patch, update permissions for the appconfigs resource.

Your Mission:

You must deploy the broken baseline architecture, analyze the Operator's logs, and fix the RBAC Role. You must grant the Operator the exact permissions required to update the status, without giving it permission to overwrite the entire Custom Resource object.

Requirements:

1. Deploy the Baseline Architecture:

You will apply two pre-written YAML manifests crd.yaml and workload.yaml present in /home/user/. The first sets up the Namespace and CRD. The second creates the my-app Custom Resource, the flawed RBAC Role/Binding, and a mock Operator Pod.

You will observe the failure by reading the logs of the mock Operator Pod.

2. Diagnose and Fix the RBAC Role:

Inspect the Role named operator-role in the rbac-lab namespace.
Currently, it only has access to resources: ["appconfigs"].
You must update this Role to explicitly include the appconfigs/status subresource, granting it the get, patch, and update verbs.
3. Verify the Reconciliation:

Once the RBAC is fixed, the mock Operator Pod will automatically succeed on its next retry loop.
Verify the CR's status has been successfully updated to "Ready".
Outcome
Mastered API Subresources by understanding the cryptographic boundary between a Custom Resource's spec (user-owned) and status (controller-owned).
Diagnosed and resolved RBAC 403 Forbidden errors resulting from missing granular privileges.
Implemented the Principle of Least Privilege in Operator design. -->

crd.yaml ->
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: rbac-lab
---
apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: appconfigs.ops.example.com
spec:
  group: ops.example.com
  names:
    kind: AppConfig
    plural: appconfigs
    singular: appconfig
  scope: Namespaced
  versions:
  - name: v1
    served: true
    storage: true
    subresources:
      status: {} # Enables the status subresource
    schema:
      openAPIV3Schema:
        type: object
        properties:
          spec:
            type: object
            properties:
              image:
                type: string
          status:
            type: object
            properties:
              phase:
                type: string
