Complete Solution
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1: Deploy the CRD and Namespace

Apply the CRD and wait a moment for it to register:

kubectl apply -f crd.yaml
sleep 3
Step 2: Deploy the Flawed Workload

Apply the workload and watch it fail with a 403 Forbidden error:

kubectl apply -f workload.yaml
kubectl logs -f mock-operator -n rbac-lab
Step 3: The Rescue (Fixing the Role)
Press Ctrl+C to exit the logs. Use your terminal to edit the live role:

kubectl edit role operator-role -n rbac-lab
Add the missing rule block so your rules section looks like this:

rules:
- apiGroups:
  - ops.example.com
  resources:
  - appconfigs
  verbs:
  - get
  - list
  - watch
  - update
  - patch
- apiGroups:
  - ops.example.com
  resources:
  - appconfigs/status
  verbs:
  - get
  - patch
  - update
Save and exit your editor.

Step 4: Verify the Fix
The mock operator pod will instantly pick up the new RBAC permissions on its next loop!

# Check the logs again to see the success message
kubectl logs mock-operator -n rbac-lab --tail=10

# Check the Custom Resource to prove the status was actually written!
kubectl get appconfig my-app -n rbac-lab -o jsonpath='{.status.phase}'